Yes. There is a whole family of these: compact phrases, gestures, or symbols that do more work than their literal content suggests. They function like semantic operators—small tokens that compress a large state transition.

* “Once upon a time…” — opens a narrative sandbox. It tells the listener to suspend ordinary temporal accounting and enter story-space.
* “Long story short…” — grants permission to discard intermediate detail and preserve only the causal spine.
* “You had to be there.” — marks the experience as poorly compressible across observers; it protects missing context from endless explanation.
* “It is what it is.” — collapses counterfactual search. Whether healthy or not, the operator says: stop recomputing alternate worlds.
* “What’s done is done.” — converts an editable problem into history.
* “For better or worse.” — precommits across unknown future states instead of evaluating each state independently.
* “Until death do us part.” — compresses thousands of future decisions into one durable rule.
* “No matter what.” — removes downstream conditions from a commitment.
* “We’ll figure it out.” — replaces an unknown solution with confidence in the future solving process.
* “Trust me.” — attempts to substitute accumulated confidence in the speaker for missing evidence in the current moment.
* “Because I said so.” — collapses justification into authority; the causal chain terminates at role.
* “Orders are orders.” — similarly converts personal deliberation into institutional delegation.
* “The customer is always right.” — compresses messy commercial disputes into a decision heuristic.
* “First, do no harm.” — makes one constraint dominate a complicated decision space.
* “Beyond a reasonable doubt.” — converts continuous uncertainty into a legally actionable threshold.
* “Innocent until proven guilty.” — establishes the default state before evidence arrives.
* “Possession is nine-tenths of the law.” — compresses a complicated dispute into the practical leverage of current control.
* “Finders keepers.” — a child-sized ownership algorithm.
* “Winner takes all.” — maps a continuous competition onto a binary allocation rule.
* “Best two out of three.” — creates a miniature error-correction protocol against one noisy trial.
* “Rock, paper, scissors.” — compresses negotiation into a tiny randomized conflict-resolution machine.
* “Call it even.” — deletes a ledger of reciprocal debts.
* “Water under the bridge.” — changes an event’s temporal category from active grievance to completed past.
* “Forgive and forget.” — attempts two operations at once: moral release plus memory deactivation.
* “Let bygones be bygones.” — instructs history not to continue exerting control over the present interaction.
* “No hard feelings.” — tries to sever outcome from relationship state.
* “Shake on it.” — transforms speech into commitment through a bodily checksum.
* A signature — compresses identity, consent, accountability, and intent into a tiny physical mark.
* A wedding ring — continuously reasserts a relational state without requiring repeated speech.
* A uniform — compresses institution, rank, expected behavior, affiliation, and authority into visible structure.
* A flag — compresses territory, history, identity, sacrifice, law, and belonging into patterned cloth.
* A crown — turns an abstract sovereignty relation into an immediately legible object.
* A badge — similarly materializes delegated authority.
* “Amen.” — closes a prayer while simultaneously signaling agreement, ratification, and surrender of further verbal addition.
* “So be it.” — takes a proposition and converts it into accepted state.
* “QED.” — tells the mathematical reader that the proof-search may terminate.
* “Case closed.” — terminates investigation.
* “Final answer.” — freezes a previously revisable candidate.
* “No further questions.” — ends an information-extraction phase.
* “Meeting adjourned.” — converts a social system from deliberative mode back into ordinary independent action.
* “Dismissed.” — changes role obligations instantly.
* “At ease.” — transforms an entire body’s posture and behavioral constraints using two words.
* “Game on.” — changes the interpretation of subsequent actions from ordinary behavior into competitive behavior.
* “Time out.” — suspends the governing rules without ending the game.
* “Reset.” — attempts to discard accumulated local state while preserving the larger system.
* “New game.” — retains the rule engine but clears the instance.
* “Checkmate.” — enormous compression: no need to play out the remaining physical captures because the future state has already become logically determined.
* “Touché.” — acknowledges a local defeat without conceding the whole argument.
* “Point taken.” — updates the model while ending the need for further persuasion on that point.
* “Enough.” — one of the purest stop operators: whatever optimization was occurring has crossed a subjective threshold.
* “Good enough.” — terminates optimization before theoretical perfection.
* “Ship it.” — converts development from optimization mode to deployment mode.
* “Mission accomplished.” — maps a multidimensional objective onto a binary completion state.
* “Return to sender.” — preserves the object/message while reversing its direction and responsibility.
* “Not my problem.” — attempts to redraw the boundary of the self’s responsibility graph.
* “My word is my bond.” — compresses contract enforcement into identity.
* “Remember who you are.” — rather than specifying an action, invokes an entire stored policy from identity.
* “Act your age.” — summons a socially expected behavioral model from a single variable.
* “Be a man.” / “Be professional.” / “Be a good sport.” — role invocation operators: load the behavioral package associated with the named archetype.
* “What would X do?” — loads another person’s inferred policy as a temporary decision engine.
* “When in Rome…” — delegates local behavior to the surrounding culture.
* “Read the room.” — tells the agent to privilege latent social state over explicit words.
* “Actions speak louder than words.” — changes evidentiary weighting from declarations toward observed behavior.
* “The exception proves the rule.” — often misused, but structurally interesting because it treats an anomalous case as information about an implicit boundary.
* “There’s no smoke without fire.” — a noisy inference shortcut: observable trace implies hidden cause.
* “Where there’s a will, there’s a way.” — compresses solution uncertainty into agency.
* “Fortune favors the bold.” — turns a probabilistic environment into an action-bias heuristic.
* “Measure twice, cut once.” — compresses the economics of reversible versus irreversible action.
* “Cross that bridge when we come to it.” — postpones computation until additional state information becomes available.
* “Don’t put the cart before the horse.” — encodes causal ordering in a visual absurdity.
* “You can’t unring a bell.” — compresses irreversibility into a physical image.
* “The die is cast.” — marks the transition from controllable decision to uncontrollable consequence.
* “Point of no return.” — names the boundary where reversal cost becomes effectively infinite.
* “And then there were none.” — compresses a whole process into terminal depletion.
* “The rest is history.” — an especially aggressive compression: everything after this point is treated as sufficiently known, inevitable, or culturally cached that narration can stop.

The deeper commonality is that these phrases often control one of a small number of hidden variables: start/stop, open/close, reversible/irreversible, uncertain/settled, editable/frozen, individual/institutional, present/past, obligation/release, simulation/action, or detail/compression.

That is probably the more useful thing to collect—not just sayings, but the operators underneath them. Once you see those, you can recognize the same structure in law, ritual, software, military commands, storytelling, contracts, games, biology, interfaces, and ordinary conversation.